<article class="env-theorem" id="0BJV">
       
      
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0BJV">Theorem <span data-tag="0BJV">51.11.6</span>.</a> Let $A$ be a Noetherian ring and let $I \subset A$ be an ideal. Set $Z = V(I) \subset \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(A)$. Let $M$ be a finite $A$-module. Set $s = s_{A, I}(M)$ as in (<a href="/tag/0BJR" data-tag="0BJR">51.11.1.1</a>). Assume that </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$A$ is universally catenary, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
the formal fibres of the local rings of $A$ are Cohen-Macaulay. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> Then $H^ i_ Z(M)$ is finite for $0 \leq i < s$ and $H^ s_ Z(M)$ is not finite. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      By Lemma <a href="/tag/0AW9" data-tag="0AW9">51.7.2</a> we may assume that $A$ is a local ring. 
    <p>
      If $A$ is a Noetherian complete local ring, then we can write $A$ as the quotient of a regular complete local ring $B$ by Cohen's structure theorem (Algebra, Theorem <a href="/tag/032A" data-tag="032A">10.160.8</a>). Using Lemma <a href="/tag/0BJS" data-tag="0BJS">51.11.2</a> and Dualizing Complexes, Lemma <a href="/tag/0BJB" data-tag="0BJB">47.9.2</a> we reduce to the case of a regular local ring which is a consequence of Lemma <a href="/tag/0BJU" data-tag="0BJU">51.11.5</a> because a regular local ring is Gorenstein (Dualizing Complexes, Lemma <a href="/tag/0AWX" data-tag="0AWX">47.21.3</a>). 
    <p>
      Let $A$ be a Noetherian local ring. Let $\mathfrak m$ be the maximal ideal. We may assume $I \subset \mathfrak m$, otherwise the lemma is trivial. Let $A^\wedge $ be the completion of $A$, let $Z^\wedge = V(IA^\wedge )$, and let $M^\wedge = M \otimes _ A A^\wedge $ be the completion of $M$ (Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/00MA" data-tag="00MA">10.97.1</a>). Then $H^ i_ Z(M) \otimes _ A A^\wedge = H^ i_{Z^\wedge }(M^\wedge )$ by Dualizing Complexes, Lemma <a href="/tag/0ALZ" data-tag="0ALZ">47.9.3</a> and flatness of $A \to A^\wedge $ (Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/00MB" data-tag="00MB">10.97.2</a>). Hence it suffices to show that $H^ i_{Z^\wedge }(M^\wedge )$ is finite for $i < s$ and not finite for $i = s$, see Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/03C4" data-tag="03C4">10.83.2</a>. Since we know the result is true for $A^\wedge $ it suffices to show that $s_{A, I}(M) = s_{A^\wedge , I^\wedge }(M^\wedge )$. This follows from Lemma <a href="/tag/0BJT" data-tag="0BJT">51.11.4</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>